## Highlights
Highlights- Nile tilapia skin ASC is similar to PSC in the physicochemical properties.- Nile tilapia skin collagens are higher thermal stability comparison to other fish.- Both are characterized by type I collagens with intact triple helical structures.- Fibrous and porous structures indicate their further use in biomaterial areas.- Nile tilapia skin collagen is an alternative source of mammalian collagen.
hydroxyproline contents of Nile tilapia ASC and PSC were 189 residues and 205 residues/1000 residues, respectively, and the rate of proline hydroxylation was found to be \(41.8\%\) and \(42.0\%\), respectively. Denaturation temperatures (Td), as measured by an Ubbelohde viscometer, were \(35.2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(34.5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively, \(6^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) lower than that of the type I collagen found in calf skin. In this study, we measured the intrinsic viscosity, circular dichroism (CD) and, X-ray diffraction (XRD), and employed Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analyses to confirm that the ASC and PSC samples from Nile tilapia skin were native and undenatured, and therefore, maintained their original, intact triple helical structure. Our SDS-PAGE results showed that the extracted ASC and PSC peptides were in their native molecular form; \((\alpha_{1})_{2}0\alpha\) (type I collagen). Furthermore, the loose, fibrous, and porous structures, shown in the cross-sections of ASC and PSC, indicate that Nile tilapia skin collagen represents a powerful physical foundation for further use in biomaterial applications.
Keywords: ASC; PSC; Characterization
## 1. Introduction
Collagen is the most abundant fibrous protein in animals, accounting for approximately \(30\%\) of total protein content [1]. Over 29 different types of collagen, each with a different amino acid sequence and biophysical properties, have been identified in a variety of tissues [2]. Of these 29, type I is the most common form.
Collagen is rich in hydroxyproline and its structure consists of three polypeptide chains twisted into a triple-helix.
The use of collagen is ubiquitous in food manufacturing and the cosmetics industry [3]. It also has several biomedical applications; these include acting as a carrier for pharmaceuticals designed for sustained release [4], and as a substrate for engineered tissue, biodegradable films [5], and other biomedical materials used in the production of wound dressings or artificial skin [6]. The versatility of collagen is the result of several characteristics, including biodegradability, biocompatibility, and weak antigenicity.
Traditionally, the primary sources of industrial and commercial collagen were limited to land-based mammals, often pig skin or bovine tendon. However, more recently, many researchers have been exploring alternative sources of collagen that can be derived from marine vertebrates or invertebrates [7, 8], particularly fish skin, scale, and bone, which are by-products of their processing. Collagen derived from marine sources is particularly valuable owing to the prevalence of various diseases in land animals, including avian flu, bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE), foot-and-mouth disease (FMD), and transmissible spongiform encephalopathy (TSE) [9], and as it bypasses the ethical and religious barriers of Muslims and Jews.
Fish by-products, especially the skin, are abundant in collagen, constituting approximately 30% of the dry weight [10]. Using fish skin as an alternative source of collagen converts this low-value raw material to a value-added product, and reduces environmental pollution. China is the biggest freshwater fish-producing nation in the
world, and tilapia farming is second only to that of carp. According to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO), China's yearly output of tilapia was approximately one million tons in 2005, accounting for nearly \(50\%\) of the worldwide yield [11]. Tilapia is normally processed as frozen fillets and exported to Europe, leaving behind large quantities of by-products, including skin and bone, which account for \(50\%\) to \(70\%\) of total fish weight. There have been few studies examining the physicochemical and biological properties of collagen from Nile tilapia skin, and these previously published studies present conflicting results regarding the characteristics of Nile tilapia skin collagen. Furthermore, few studies have systematically compared acid-soluble (ASC) and pepsin-soluble (PSC) collagen from Nile tilapia skin. Therefore, the aim of this study is to extract ASC and PSC from Nile tilapia skin, describe their physical and chemical properties, and illustrate the differences and similarities in the characteristics of these peptides.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Raw materials
The descaled Nile tilapia skins were provided by Lushi Aquatic Product Company (Guangzhou, Guangdong province, China). The fish skins were frozen and transported with cold chain logistics until being sent to our laboratory. The fish skins were thawed with running water. The residuals under skins were removed and the skins were rinsed with pre-cooling deionized water. The cleaned fish skins were cut into small pieces (approximately \(5 \times 5\) mm) and stored at \(-20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until used for collagen extraction.
Pepsin (1:3000 U) was purchased from Solarbio Company. All other reagents used
were of analytical grade.
## 2.2. Preparation of ASC
The following operating steps were performed on ice or at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) as previously reported by Senaratne et al. [12] with some modification. The processed pieces of Nile tilapia skins were thawed and stirred (78-1, Danrui Experimental Instrument Equipment Co., China) constantly with 20 volumes (v/m) of 0.1 M NaOH for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to eliminate non-collagenous proteins and other impurities and to get rid of the impact of endogenous proteases on collagen. The alkaline solution was changed twice. The fish skins treated with alkali were neutralized by washing repeatedly with pre-cooling deionized water and then extracted by \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid in a ratio of 1:55 (w/v) with a gentle stirring. It is necessary to avoid rapid agitation during the process of extraction in order to prevent the collagen fibrils from being entangled. The extract was then centrifuged (2-16 KL, Sigma, USA) at \(9500\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Following centrifugation, the supernatants collected were salted-out with grinded NaCl at a final concentration of \(0.9\,\mathrm{M}\) and left overnight. Then the precipitate was obtained by centrifugation at \(9500\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and dissolved in \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid. The resulting solution was dialyzed against \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) and replaced with deionized water until the electrical conductivity of dialysate was close to that of deionized water. The both dialysates were renewed every \(8\;\mathrm{h}\). The lyophilized (VSP-62, Marin Christ Co., Germany) ASC samples were sealed in polythene bags and stored in a dryer until used for subsequent analysis.
2.3. Preparation of PSC
The procedures were identical with the preparation of ASC except that the fish skins were extracted by 50 volumes (v/w) of \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid containing \(0.5\%\) (w/w) pepsin and dialyzed against \(0.02\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{HPO}_{4}\) until the pH value was bigger than 8.0 to inactivate pepsin before dialyzed against \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid.
## 2.4. Amino acid analysis
The lyophilized collagen samples were hydrolyzed with \(6\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{HCl}\) at \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 24 h and then vaporized. The hydrolysates were dissolved in a \(25\,\mathrm{mL}\) citric acid buffer and determined in Analysis and Test Center, Institute of Oceanology, Chinese Academy of Sciences using an amino acid analyzer (L-8900, Hitachi, Japan).
For determination of hydroxyproline only, collagen samples were analyzed by the method of Woessner [13] with some modification. The L-hydroxyproline standard (predried to constant weight) was dissolved in \(0.001\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{HCl}\) to prepare the stock solution of \(1\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\) and then diluted to a final concentration of \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) before used. Hydroxyproline standard solution series were prepared. The collagen samples \((5\,\mathrm{mg})\) were weighed accurately, sealed into ampoule bottles and hydrolyzed in \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) \(6\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{HCl}\) at \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). Then the cooled digestive juices were removed, and the ampoule bottles were washed several times with distilled water. Then all digestive solutions were transferred into \(100\,\mathrm{mL}\) volumetric flasks. The solution was titrated into pink with \(6\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{NaOH}\) with phenol phthalein as indicator and brought to volume with distilled water. Then \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) of the sample solutions adding \(1.5\,\mathrm{mL}\) absolute ethanol and standard solutions were transferred into \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) colorimetric tubes, mixed with \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) chloramine T and stood at room temperature for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\). And \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) perchloric acid was added to
destroy chloramine T. Five minutes later, each colorimetric tube was added to \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) para-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde, mixed quickly, placed in water bath at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 20 min, and then cooled with running water. The absorbance of samples and standard solution series was measured at \(560\;\mathrm{nm}\) using zero tube as the blank.
2.5. Determination of denaturation temperature (Td)
The denaturation temperature of collagen was analyzed, based on the changes of viscosity measured with an Ubbelohde viscometer, according to the method reported by Zhang et al. [14] with a slight modification. Viscosity were obtained at several temperatures from \(10^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) raised at an interval of \(3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(43^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Fractional viscosity at specific temperature was calculated according to the following equation:
Fractional viscosity \(=\left(\eta_{\mathrm{sp}}(T)-\eta_{\mathrm{sp}}(43^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\right)/\left(\eta_{\mathrm{sp}}(10^{\circ}\mathrm{C})-\eta_{\mathrm{sp}}(43^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\right)\)
Where \(\eta_{\mathrm{sp}}\) is the specific viscosity, specific viscosity \(=(t-t_{0})/t_{0}=\eta_{\mathrm{r}}-1\), where \(\eta_{\mathrm{r}}\) is the relative viscosity, relative viscosity \(=t/t_{0}\), where \(t\) is the time of collagen sample solutions passing through capillary, and \(t_{0}\) is the time of solvent passing through capillary at the same temperature. The thermal determination curve was obtained by fractional viscosities against the temperatures and the denaturation temperature of collagen was the temperature where fractional viscosity was 0.5.
## 2.6. Determination of intrinsic viscosity
The collagen intrinsic viscosity was analyzed by the method of Ubbelohde viscometer. The collagen samples were dissolved in \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid to a concentration of \(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\), and then diluted into \(5/6\), \(2/3\), \(5/9\), \(1/2\), \(1/3\), \(1/4~\mathrm{mg/mL}\), respectively. The time that these solutions passed through capillary at \(10^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was measured and recorded.
According to the following equation:
\[
[\eta]=\operatorname*{lim}_{c\to0}\eta\mathrm{sp}/c
\]
Where \( c \) was the concentration of sample solution, \( \eta_{\mathrm{sp}}/c \) were plotted against \( c \), and the intercept was referred to as intrinsic viscosity \( ([\eta], \mathrm{dL}/\mathrm{g}) \) when \( c \to 0 \).
2.7. Circular dichroism (CD) spectra
The collagen samples were dissolved in \( 0.5\,\mathrm{M} \) acetic acid to obtain a concentration of \( 0.25\,\mathrm{mg/mL} \) and centrifuged at \( 10{,}000\,\mathrm{rpm} \) for \( 30\,\mathrm{min} \) at \( 4^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). The spectra of resulting supernatants were recorded from the wavelength of 300 to \( 200\,\mathrm{nm} \) (Jasco J-815, Japan Spectroscopic Co., Japan).
## 2.8. X-ray diffraction (XRD)
The X-ray diffraction (X' Pert Pro MPD, Anton Paar, Austria) of collagen samples were analyzed by Qingdao Institute of Biomass Energy and Bioprocess Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences. The wavelength of its radiation was \( \lambda_{\mathrm{Cukα1}} = 1.5406\,\mathrm{\AA} \). It scanned the 2θ interval between 4 and \( 45^{\circ} \) by a step of \( 0.02^{\circ} \) and an angular speed of \( 4^{\circ}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1} \).
## 2.9. Fourier transforms infrared spectroscopy (FTIR)
\( 1\,\mathrm{mg} \) lyophilized collagen sample was mixed approximately at 1:100 ratio with dried potassium bromide (KBr) (spectrum pure) and pressed into pellets under dry conditions. The infrared spectra (iS 10, Thermo-Nicolet Co., USA) were recorded in the \( 4000{-}400\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) range at the resolution of \( 1\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) for a single scan. The peak wavelength values were analyzed with the OMNIC 8.0 software.
2.10. Sodium dodecyl sulphate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE)
SDS-PAGE was carried out on the basis of the method mentioned by Laemmli [15] with \(7.5\%\) resolving gel and \(5\%\) stacking gel. The sample loading buffer was used in the presence or absence of \(\beta\)-Me. Sykes reduction method was done according to the description of Wu et al. [16]. The molecular weights of the subunits were analyzed by the location of bands.
## 2.11. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM)
The morphology of collagen samples were observed in Medical College, Qingdao University. The collagen samples were pasted on a blade with double side adhesive tape in the desired orientation. Then they were placed inside the sputter for gold coating and the sputtered specimens were observed with scanning microscope (JSM-840, JEOL, Japan) at \(50\times\), \(200\times\), and \(400\times\) magnification.
## 2.12. Statistical analysis
Three replicates were measured for each sample. All quantitative results were shown as the average \(\pm\) standard deviation.
## 3. Results and discussion
3.1. Correlation between amino acid composition and thermal denaturation temperatures of ASC and PSC
The amino acid compositions of ASC and PSC, expressed as residues per 1000 amino acids, are presented in Table 1. The most abundant amino acids found in these collagens were glycine, alanine, proline, and hydroxyproline. The glycine content of ASC and PSC (338 residues/1000 residues, 333 residues/1000 residues, respectively), account for approximately one-third of the total amino acid residues. This high glycine
content shows that the (Gly-X-Y)\(_n\) repeats in collagen polypeptide chains form a triple helix structure. No tryptophan residues were found. Although cysteine residues were found in the ASC and PSC of Nile tilapia skin, and have also been shown to be a component of the collagen from carp scales, previous studies have shown it is not a component of collagen extracted from porcine dermis [17] or carp muscles [18]. It was likely related to the existence of type III collagen. Because the main types of collagen in the skin of aquatic animals were type I and type III. Type III might be incorporated in the process of the extraction of type I collagen, which induced the impure extracted type I collagen. The characteristic of type III collagen was that it had many disulfide linkages, which indicated many cysteines incorporated in type III collagen. However, it was found to be very low in cysteine (0.2%, 0.3%, respectively), lower than that of PSC from Nile tilapia reported previously (1%) [19]; which indicated that the extracted collagen samples had high purity.
The proline and hydroxyproline contents of the ASC and PSC were 189 residues and 205 residues/1000 residues, respectively, which is similar to that of carp skin collagen (192 residues) [20], but higher than cod skin (154 residues) and lower than porcine skin (220 residues) [17] and bovine skin (215 residues) [21]. The rate of proline hydroxylation (hydroxyproline content/the sum of proline and hydroxyproline contents) was 41.8% for ASC and 42.0% for PSC, similar to the 43% found in channel catfish [22], although it is approximately 6% less than cuttlefish [23].
Although the transition curves of ASC and PSC are similar, with Td values approximately 35.2°C and 34.5°C, respectively, the specific viscosity of ASC is
significantly higher than that of PSC (Fig. 1). However, these results differ from the PSC Td values measured by differential scanning calorimetry in Nile tilapia \((32.09^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) [19] and \(O\). niloticas \((28^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) [24]. These conflicting Td values may be the result of the different methods applied or tilapia species examined. Moreover, the Td values were higher than those from many marine fishes, especially deep-sea fishes such as the Alaskan pollack \((16.8^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) [25], and lower by about \(6^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) than that of type I collagen from calf skin. This is likely related to the different amino acid composition and environment [17]. Furthermore, these results indicate that the triple helix structure of collagen from fresh water fishes is more stable than that of marine fishes.
Although the ASC and PSC have similar Td values, their amino acid compositions are different (Table 1). These results agree with those reported by Zhang et al. [14], who showed that the Td value of ASC has a lower hydroxyproline content, and is higher than that of PSC. In general, a higher rate of proline hydroxylation results in a higher Td value and better thermal stability. It strengthens the triple helix structure of collagen thanks to the formation of intramolecular hydrogen bonds [26] and pyrrole rings [27]. 3.2. Intrinsic viscosity of ASC and PSC
The intrinsic viscosity curves were derived from the specific viscosities and concentrations of the sample solutions (Fig. 2). Ciarlo et al. [28] reported that the intrinsic viscosity of collagen with a triple helix structure at low temperatures (below the denaturation temperature) is \(11.5\;\mathrm{dL/g}\) or \(13.4 \pm 0.9\;\mathrm{dL/g}\), which is characteristic of rod-shaped molecules. The intrinsic viscosity of a random coil ranges from 0.1–1.5 \(\mathrm{dL/g}\), whereas compressed spherical particles range from 0.05–0.2 \(\mathrm{dL/g}\), which is in
line with studies showing that the intrinsic viscosity of gelatin and collagen hydrolysates are 0.5174 dL/g, 0.0641 dL/g, respectively [29].
As shown in Fig. 2, the ratio of the specific viscosity to concentration for ASC and PSC has a linear relationship to the concentration of the collagen solutions given by \( y=46.181x+14.069 \) (\( R^{2}=0.9544 \)) and \( y=23.262x+12.817 \) (\( R^{2}=0.991 \)), respectively, and the intrinsic viscosity of ASC and PSC is 14.069 dL/g and 12.817 dL/g, respectively. These results are consistent with a study by Montero et al. [30], who showed that rod-shaped collagen extracted with acetic acid does not change the triple helix structure. These results also show that the ASC and PSC samples retain their native form and maintain their original triple helix structure.
## 3.3. Secondary structure of ASC and PSC
The secondary structures of ASC and PSC were determined by CD and XRD. CD was performed to confirm that the extracted ASC and PSC samples were in their native states. CD is an effective method to identify whether the triple helical structure is intact. It measures the differences in absorption of left-rotated and right-rotated circularly polarized light. In the far-ultraviolet region, the CD spectrum of collagen adopts a polyproline II-like helical conformation with a positive peak at 221 nm (maximum positive cotton effect) and a negative peak at 198 nm (maximum negative cotton effect), and a crossover point (zero rotation) at approximately 213 nm [31]. These values are characteristic of native collagen [32]. The ratio of the positive peak to the negative peak (Rpn) is unique to the structural conformation, therefore, it can be used to identify the triple helical conformation [33]. Feng et al. [33] reported the Rpn of collagen with a
triple helical structure ranged from 0.12–0.15. As shown in Fig. 3 (i), the CD spectra of both ASC and PSC exhibit significantly weak positive peaks (221 nm), strong negative peaks (197 nm), and a crossover point at nearly 213.5 nm. These values are similar to those for black drum and sheepshead seabream skin ASC and PSC (220–221 nm, 197–199 nm, respectively) [1]. The Nile tilapia skin ASC and PSC Rpn value is 0.12 and 0.13, respectively, indicating a triple helical conformation. There was no disappearance of the positive peak, nor was there a red shift of the negative peak, both of which are seen in the random coil conformation of gelatin and collagen hydrolysates. This indicates that both ASC and PSC maintained the integrity of the triple helical structure. Therefore, these results show that the extraction process did not damage the triple helical conformation in either of the collagen samples.
The XRD diagrams are shown in Fig. 3 (ii). Both samples had two peaks each, characteristic of collagen, at diffraction angles (2θ) of \(7.57^{\circ}\) and \(21.25^{\circ}\), and \(7.42^{\circ}\) and \(20.23^{\circ}\), respectively. The first sharp peak is associated with the triple helical structure of collagen. The Bragg equation \(\mathrm{d}(\mathring{\mathrm{A}})=\lambda/2\sin\theta\) (where \(\lambda\) is the X-ray wavelength (1.54 Å), and \(\theta\) is the Bragg diffraction angle), was used to calculate the minimum value of the repeated spacings [34]. The d values of the first relatively sharp peak, indicating the distance between the molecular chains, were 11.66 Å and 11.90 Å, and those of the second broad peak, reflecting the distance between skeletons, were 4.18 Å and 4.38 Å, similar to the PSC values measured in carp scale [14]. These results correspond to the diameter of a collagen molecule with a triple helix structure and a single left-handed helix chain. Therefore, both extracted collagen samples were in their native
conformations and undenatured.
## 3.4. FTIR analyses
Characteristic peaks corresponding to the major absorption bands in the FTIR spectra for ASC and PSC are shown in Fig. 3 (iii). These peaks correspond to five main amide bonds, including amide A, amide B, amide I, amide II, and amide III, indicative of the amino acid composition and high proportion of proline and hydroxyproline in the collagen molecule. The FTIR spectrum of ASC and PSC were identical, which is consistent with the findings of Zhang et al. [35]. The amide A bands of ASC and PSC were measured at \(3311.39\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(3314.59\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively. These values are commonly associated with N-H stretching frequency and indicate the presence of hydrogen bonds. According to Doyle et al. [36], a free N-H stretching vibration ranges from 3400 to \(3440\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), but shifts lower to approximately \(3300\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) when hydrogen bonds are introduced. The amide B bands of both ASC and PSC were measured at \(2936.65\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), consistent with \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) asymmetrical stretching [37]. The amide I bands of ASC and PSC were measured at \(1658.72\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), with a characteristic wavelength value in the range of \(1600{-}1700\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is associated with carbonyl group stretching vibration coupled to a carboxyl group. The amide II bands of ASC and PSC were measured at \(1546.62\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1543.42\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively, which can be attributed to a N-H bending vibration and a C-N stretching vibration. Finally, the amide III band values (\(1235.94\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1232.74\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively) represent the combination of the C-N stretching vibration and the N-H bending vibration [38]. Furthermore, the absorption ratio, derived from the amino III and \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) bending bands (\(1447.33\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for
ASC and \(1450.53\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) for PSC), was approximately 1.0, confirming the presence of an intact triple helical structure [37, 39]. In summary, the CD, XRD, and FTIR analyses confirm that the extracted ASC and PSC samples retained their native, intact triple helical structure.
## 3.5. Subunit composition of ASC and PSC
SDS-PAGE results confirmed the ASC and PSC (Fig. 4) contain several \(\upgamma\) chains (trimer), many \(\upbeta\) chains (dimer), and at least two distinct \(\mathfrak{a}\) chains, with variable mobility (\(\mathbf{a}_{1}\) and \(\mathfrak{a}_{2}\)). Their molecular weights were approximately 125 and \(114\ \mathrm{kDa}\), respectively. The banding pattern of ASC was similar to PSC despite the excision of the telopeptide region, which did not affect the integrity of the triple helical structure [1]. The density of \(\upgamma\) chains in ASC was notably higher than in PSC, however, PSC had a slightly lower \(\mathfrak{a}\) chain density, indicating that ASC contains more crosslinks. In addition, there was no significant difference in the banding pattern, even with the inclusion of \(\upbeta\)-Me, indicating the absence of disulfide bonds, confirming that these extracted collagens contain very little sulfur-containing amino acids (Table 1).
A study by Kimura et al. [25] showed there are two distinct collagen heterotrimers, \((\mathfrak{a}_{1})_{2}\mathfrak{a}_{2}\) and \(\mathfrak{a}_{1}\mathfrak{a}_{2}\mathfrak{a}_{3}\), in the skin of tilapia, and that type I collagen is the most abundant [40]. However, these results could not confirm whether both of the collagens examined contain the \(\mathfrak{a}_{3}\) chain based on the SDS-PAGE lane 2–5 results, because the \(\mathfrak{a}_{1}(\mathrm{I})\) chain could not be separated from \(\mathfrak{a}_{3}(\mathrm{I})\) chain due to similar peptide mobility. However, the \(\mathfrak{a}_{1}(\mathrm{I})\) chain should be separable from the \(\mathfrak{a}_{3}(\mathrm{I})\) chain according to Sykes reduction method, if the \(\mathfrak{a}_{3}(\mathrm{I})\) chain is present [16]. As shown in lanes 6 and 7, there was also one
\(\upbeta\) chain and two different \(\mathfrak{a}\) chains besides the \(\mathfrak{a}_{3}\) chain. Moreover, the \(\mathfrak{a}_{1}\) chain band is approximately 2 fold wider than that of the \(\mathfrak{a}_{2}\) chain, which indicates that the ASC and PSC from Nile tilapia skin are in the \((\mathfrak{a}_{1})_{2}\mathfrak{a}_{2}\) conformation, classified as type I collagen, which is consistent with collagens extracted from the skin of carp [2] and channel catfish [22].
## 3.6. Morphology
SEM images (at \(50\times\), \(200\times\), and \(400\times\) magnification) of cross-sectioned, lyophilized ASC and PSC are shown in Fig. 5. The superficial morphology of ASC was similar to that of PSC; white, uniform, and pyknotic. The SEM microstructures of both collagens displayed a complex, multi-layered, aggregated fibrous meshwork appearance. The loose, fibrous, and porous structure was clearly visible at \(50\times\) magnification. These regions were also observed as large filamentous flakes, arranged in parallel orientations, at \(200\times\) and \(400\times\) magnifications.
Recently, collagen derived from various by-products of the food processing industry has been shown to be valuable in several biomedical areas, with diverse medical applications. Understanding its microstructure is critical to understanding its biomedical potential as a physical foundation for future biomedical uses. Porosity, fibril shape, and interconnectivity are critical parameters in biomaterial evaluation. These SEM results indicate the ASC and PSC from Nile tilapia skin are suitable for biomedical engineering. The complex porous and fibrillar network structures of both collagens could be utilized as hydrating agents, in wound dressings, as matrices for cell proliferation, or for other essential biomedical applications [40].
## 4. Conclusions
4. ConclusionsThe physicochemical properties of collagen differ from one source to another. There is a large quantity of Nile tilapia skin, a by-product of processing, which could serve as a valuable source for collagen extraction and utilization. These results show that there was no notable difference in the physicochemical properties of Nile tilapia skin ASC and PSC. Both collagens are typical I collagens. The CD, XRD, and FTIR analyses indicate that their triple helical structures remained intact following extraction. In conclusion, this study demonstrated that Nile tilapia skin is an excellent source of collagen, which has the potential to replace mammalian sources, and both ASC and PSC extracted from Nile tilapia skin can be used as raw materials in food and cosmetic preparation. However, further study is needed to determine whether either is suitable for use in biomedical applications.
